  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n- Wet and windy system Thursday through Friday\n\nA few showers will remain possible early this morning before \nending/moving south of the forecast area by daybreak. The vast \nmajority of the convection is already south of the area closer to \nthe sfc boundary where the better instability is found. The short \nwave that is helping to fuel these showers and storms will be \nshifting east of the area by 12z this morning. We are expecting a \nconsiderable amount of cloud cover to hold much of the day as plenty \nof low level moisture will advect south from Canada behind the short \nwave. Holes in the clouds should develop as the clouds lift with \ndiurnal mixing helping a bit.\n\nOur attention then turns to the phasing system that has generally \nbeen handled well quite consistently by the models for days now. The \nsouthern stream part of this system will push showers and a few \nstorms over the area beginning mid to late Thursday. This will be \nassociated with a sfc low that is forecast to ride through our \nforecast area Thursday evening. \n\nThu evening will be the best chance for better rain and the best \nchance of storms. This is when we see the best instability approach \nthe area via the best moisture push associated with the nose of a 50 \nknot low level jet clipping the far southern portion of the area. \nInstability progs show that we likely do not see sfc based \ninstability this far north, but we do see sufficient elevated \ninstability. This setup should keep most, if not all of the severe \npotential south.\n\nWe may see a brief break in the rain as the southern feature moves \nout by Friday morning, and the northern stream trough is still to \nour NW. The upper jet core crosses the forecast area Friday \nafternoon, bringing the cyclonic flow overhead at that time. This is \ndelayed a bit compared to earlier runs, and is likely to hold \nrain/showers over the area longer on Friday. The showers will be the \nresult of the deep cold pool aloft with diurnal instability, and \nlake effect. The best chance of showers will be Friday afternoon \ninto Friday evening until the upper jet core on the backside passes \neast. \n\nWe are looking at a legit chance of lake effect thunderstorms Friday \nafternoon and evening. Forecast soundings modified for lake effect \nshow lifted parcels way above the ice generation layer, allowing for \nlightning to be generated. The thunder chance will be limited to \nmainly the lakeshore. All of this will be accompanied by winds of 35 \nto 45 mph bringing in the much colder air.\n  \n- Cool and dry weekend with moderating temperatures next week\n\nBy Saturday morning, sfc ridging will be over the area already as \nthe upper trough moves by and we are downstream of the next long \nwave ridge. This ridge will become a familiar feature as it is \nexpected to hold over the area through early next week. It gets held \nup with the strongly amplified pattern with strong lows near both \ncoasts of the CONUS. \n\nWe start off quite cool in the wake of Friday's system for the \nweekend. Temperatures may end up in the 50s only once again on \nSaturday. We will see a gradual warming trend then through early \nnext week with dry weather. A better push of warmer air comes around \nTuesday as return flow brings a warm front toward the area. This \ncould bring a small chance of some rain.",
